  "robust_conclusions": [
    "A dominant long period near 108-110 ka is recovered by every analyst choice that keeps binned series longer than ~30 points: raw, 2.5, 5 and 10 ka bins all give 108.8-109.5 ka, and all three missing-data treatments and three of four detrending variants give 108.4-108.9 ka. The period is stable to +-0.5 ka, which is 66x tighter than the record can actually resolve.",
    "The 108-110 ka peak beats an AR(1) red-noise null matched to the record's OWN lag-1 autocorrelation at the raw 0.46 ka sampling step (rho = 0.788): empirical p = 0.0050 with 200 surrogates and the same 40-300 ka scan on each surrogate, so the look-elsewhere effect is priced in. The cycling is not white or red noise at this autocorrelation.",
    "The period estimate is insensitive to the 28 missing values: complete-case (n=593), linear interpolation and mean imputation give 108.83, 108.90 and 108.64 ka -- a maximum change of 0.26 ka, 128x smaller than the 33.2 ka resolution limit.",
    "Amplitude is a stable fraction of the series' own spread and a stable absolute size: the fundamental amplitude is 0.36-0.39 per mille PDB and 0.97-1.13 of the binned series sd for bin widths of 0-10 ka, so smoothing does not create or destroy the cycle's amplitude.",
    "The 217 ka harmonic is present in every scan but is always the weaker of the two low-frequency peaks (R^2 0.41 vs 0.43 raw), consistent with the second harmonic of a ~108 ka cycle rather than an independent 217 ka cycle -- and the two are separated by 108 ka, more than the 33 ka resolution limit, so this is a resolvable statement."
  ],
  "non_robust_conclusions": [
    "Which local maximum is 'the dominant period' FLIPS at 20 ka bins: the best period moves from 108.8 ka (raw) to 48.7 ka (20 ka bins, R^2 0.86) because 20 ka bins leave only 17 points, so the scan-search look-elsewhere effect inflates R^2 without adding evidence. The 20 ka result is an artefact of effective sample size, not a shorter cycle.",
    "R^2 is NOT a robust quantity and its ranking depends on the detrending convention. Intercept-only versus quadratic differ by only 0.025 in period (108.51 vs 108.83 ka) but the quadratic variant REPORTED best period jumps to 217.4 ka (R^2 0.477) because the quadratic trend and the ~217 ka harmonic are nearly collinear. Against a common (linearly detrended) sum of squares the four variants span 0.42-0.46, so part of any 'detrending matters' claim is an accounting convention.",
    "The R^2 rise from 0.43 (raw) to 0.64 (10 ka bins) is smoothing suppressing noise between 0.065 and 10 ka, not the cycle becoming stronger; the signal-to-noise reading of R^2 therefore depends entirely on the chosen bin width.",
    "Significance is a choice too: against its own AR(1) null only the raw sampling gives p <= 0.05 (p = 0.0050); the binned variants do not (1/5 variants significant; 2.5 ka p = 0.25, 20 ka p = 0.07) because high lag-1 autocorrelation at coarse bin steps lets the null produce R^2 0.49-0.70 at random. Binning for visual smoothness destroys the very evidence that makes the peak significant.",
    "Sub-resolution structure near the peak is unresolvable: the smallest period difference the record can resolve at ~109 ka is P^2/T = 33.2 ka, and the Lomb-Scargle sidelobes at 79 ka, 63 ka plus the spectral-window sidelobes of the age sampling at 76 ka, 62 ka are only 30 ka away -- they fall INSIDE the resolution limit, so the 97/108/119 ka triplet seen in step 02 is one broad peak, not three cycles."
  ],
  "explanation": "Rows are not independent replicates, so no p-value here treats rows as independent. R^2 values are in-sample fit statistics of the stated design; the only inferential number is the AR(1) Monte-Carlo null (200 surrogates per smoothing width, matched to that variant's own lag-1 autocorrelation at its bin step, using the same period scan so the look-elsewhere effect is included). Part 1 bins d18o by non-overlapping consecutive age bins of the listed width (trailing partial bin dropped) after dropping the 28 missing values, then fits [1, t, cos, sin, cos(2wt), sin(2wt)] over 40-300 ka in 0.065 ka steps; n_bins = number of points for the raw (0 ka) case. Amplitude is the fundamental harmonic amplitude hypot(b_cos, b_sin) from that fit. Part 2 keeps the raw sampling and varies the trend part of the design: (a) intercept only, (b) +t, (c) +t and +t^2, (d) linear trend fitted and subtracted before the sinusoid fit (R^2 then uses the detrended series' own total sum of squares). Part 3 repeats the baseline design on the three missing-data treatments. Part 4 gives the record's resolution limit: n_cycles = T/P_best = 3.27 and Rayleigh frequency resolution 1/T = 0.00281 /ka, so the smallest resolvable period difference at the best period is P^2/T = 33.2 ka; Lomb-Scargle local maxima near the main peak and the spectral-window sidelobes of the age sampling are both listed with their offsets in units of 1/T."
}
